Problem 1 — Sequential Processing
Suppose a sentence has

100 words.

RNN processes like this.
Word 1

↓

Word 2

↓

Word 3

↓

...

↓

Word 100

Question

Can it jump directly to Word 100?

No.

It must process

99 words first.
Why is this a problem?
Modern GPUs can process many computations at the same time.

RNN cannot fully use this advantage because each word depends on the previous hidden state.

This makes training slower.

Interview Question ⭐
Why is RNN slow?
Answer
RNN processes words sequentially. Each word depends on the hidden state from the previous word, so computations cannot be fully parallelized. This increases training time, especially for long sequences.

Problem 2 — Forgetting Earlier Information
This is called the
Long-Range Dependency Problem
Example
Krishna,
who studied AI for many years,
worked on multiple projects,
completed several certifications,
joined a new company,
because she worked very hard.

Question

Who worked hard?

Answer
Krishna

But "Krishna" appeared a long time ago.

The RNN memory becomes weaker as the sentence grows.

Sometimes it forgets.
Another Example
The animal didn't cross the road because it was tired.

Question

Who was tired?

Animal?

Road?

Humans immediately answer

Animal.

RNN may struggle because "animal" appeared much earlier.
What is Long-Range Dependency?
Definition
Long-range dependency occurs when understanding a word depends on another word that is far away in the sentence.

This is a common interview definition.
Why Was LSTM Invented?
Researchers realized
RNN forgets too much.

So they improved it.
What is LSTM?
LSTM = Long Short-Term Memory

It is an improved version of RNN.

Its goal:

Remember important information for a longer time.

Forget unnecessary information.
Real-Life Analogy
Suppose today you learned
- Python
- Your lunch menu
- Your friend's birthday
After one year

Which will you remember?

Probably
- Python
- Birthday
You forget

Lunch menu.

LSTM works similarly.
How LSTM Does This
Instead of one memory,

it has gates.

Think of them as security guards.

They decide:
Keep

Forget

Update

These gates help LSTM retain important information longer than a standard RNN.
Did LSTM Solve Everything?
No.

It improved memory,

but it still had major problems.
Problems with LSTM
1. Still Sequential
Word1

↓

Word2

↓

Word3

Still one by one.
2. Still Slow
Because of sequential processing.
3. Long Documents Are Still Difficult
Even with gates,

very long contexts remain challenging.
4. Hard to Train
Long sequences require more computation and make optimization more difficult.
Interview Question ⭐
Why is LSTM better than RNN?
Answer
LSTM introduces memory cells and gates that decide what information to keep, forget, or update. 
This helps preserve important information over longer sequences and reduces the vanishing gradient problem compared to a standard RNN.

Then Came Attention
Researchers asked:
Why should the model try to remember everything?

Instead,

what if

the model simply looks back whenever needed?

This idea became
Attention
Instead of remembering every word,

the model focuses only on the most relevant words.
Then Came Transformer
Google published

Attention Is All You Need (2017)

Their idea was revolutionary.

Instead of sequential memory,

let every word communicate directly with every other word.

This became the
Transformer Architecture
