RNN vs LSTM vs Transformer

| Feature | RNN | LSTM | Transformer |
|---------|-----|------|-------------|
| Reads words | One by one | One by one | All at once (parallel) |
| Memory | Hidden State | Hidden State + Gates | Self-Attention |
| Long context | Poor | Better | Excellent |
| Parallel processing | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ |
| Training speed | Slow | Slow | Fast |
| Used in modern LLMs | Rarely | Rarely | Yes |

Why Modern LLMs Use Transformers
Models like:
- GPT
- BERT
- LLaMA
- Gemini
- Claude
all use the Transformer architecture because it:
- Understands long context better.
- Trains efficiently on GPUs.
- Processes tokens in parallel.
- Captures relationships between any two words using Self-Attention.


Interview Questions (Part 0)
Q1. What is an RNN?
Answer:
RNN, or Recurrent Neural Network, is a neural network designed for sequential data. It processes one element at a time and maintains a hidden state to carry information from previous inputs.

Q2. What is the hidden state in an RNN?
Answer:
The hidden state is the RNN's memory. It stores information from previously processed words and passes it to the next step so the model can use past context.

Q3. What are the limitations of an RNN?
Answer:
- Sequential processing
- Slow training
- Difficulty remembering long-range dependencies
- Vanishing gradient problem
Q4. Why was LSTM introduced?
Answer:
LSTM was introduced to address the memory limitations of RNNs. It uses memory cells and gates to decide what information to keep, forget, or update, enabling it to preserve important information over longer sequences.

Q5. Why wasn't LSTM enough?
Answer:
Although LSTM improves memory, it still processes words sequentially, making training slower and limiting efficient parallel computation. It can also struggle with very long contexts.

Q6. Why was the Transformer introduced?
Answer:
The Transformer was introduced to overcome the sequential processing limitations of RNNs and LSTMs. By using Self-Attention, it allows every token to directly attend to every other token while enabling parallel processing, making it faster and more effective for long sequences.

Q7. Why do modern LLMs use Transformers?
Answer:
Modern LLMs use Transformers because they can process sequences in parallel, capture long-range dependencies effectively through Self-Attention, and scale efficiently to very large datasets and models.

